\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Different torsion classes with the same matter operator}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{On the 4-site integral torus of \ref{fdip-00YP}, set all
#{R_i(x)=1}. Compare zero translation labels with labels having
#{a_1(0,0)=e_1} and all other positive labeled edge values zero.
For the square with attaching walk
##{(0,0)\longrightarrow(1,0)\longrightarrow(1,1)
 \longrightarrow(0,1)\longrightarrow(0,0),}
the rotational holonomy is #{1}. The translation is the signed
sum of the 4 edge labels, hence is #{0} in the first
assignment and #{e_1} in the second. The quotient of
\ref{fdip-00Z1} is #{V}, so these torsion classes are distinct
over #{\mathbb Z} and #{\mathbb Q}. Nevertheless
\ref{fdip-00Z2} gives identical rotation-only operators and
identical mass-shifted determinants on the same rank-#{64} module.}
